window.AG_PRACTICE["bencao"] = {"id":"bencao","axis":"herb","seal":"草","scene":"art/scene-garden.jpg","title":"Herbs Easily Confused","titleZh":"本草辨異","sub":"Pairs that share a name, a plant, or a job — and part company in clinic","subZh":"同名、同株、同功，而臨證分道者","mentor":{"key":"yao","nm":"Yào Wēng","nmZh":"藥翁","line":"\"Nobody confuses ginseng with rhubarb. What costs you marks — and costs a patient a fortnight — is the pair that look like the same herb until you ask one more question.\"","lineZh":"「人參與大黃，無人相混。教你失分、教病人白費半個月的，是那些看起來像同一味藥、直到你多問一句才分得開的兩味。」"},"card":{"h":"Ask the Second Question 再問一句","hZh":"","p":"Most confusable pairs separate on one axis. Fresh or dried changes ginger from an exterior-releasing herb into an interior-warming one. Raw or steamed turns Rehmannia from cooling blood to filling essence. Twig or bark decides whether Cinnamon opens the surface or warms the root. Strength decides between Ginseng and Codonopsis; direction decides between Astragalus and Ginseng. Find the axis and the pair falls apart cleanly.","pZh":"易混之對，多決於一軸。生與乾，使薑由解表之品轉為溫裡之品；生與熟，使地黃由涼血轉為填精；枝與皮，定桂之開表抑或溫裡。人參與黨參，別在其力；黃耆與人參，別在其向。得其軸，則其對自判。"},"items":[{"k":"mc","q":"Fresh Ginger and Dried Ginger come from the same plant. What changes?","qZh":"生薑與乾薑同出一物，所異者何？","opts":[{"t":"Fresh releases the exterior and stops nausea; dried warms the middle, rescues devastated yang and warms the Lung to transform thin mucus","c":"生薑發散風寒、溫中止嘔；乾薑溫中回陽、溫肺化飲","ok":1,"note":"Fresh is acrid, slightly warm and outward-moving — the kitchen herb for an early wind-cold and for vomiting. Dried is acrid, hot and inward-moving; it stays in the middle burner, and in Sini Tang it goes to the rescue of collapsed yang. Same plant, opposite direction of travel. 生者辛微溫而性走散，主風寒初起與嘔吐，廚下之常品。乾者辛熱而性守中，四逆湯用之以回陽救逆。同物而所趨相反。"},{"t":"They are interchangeable; the drying is only for storage","c":"二者可以互代，乾之但為久藏","ok":0,"note":"Substituting one for the other changes what the formula does. A patient with an early wind-cold given dried ginger has been warmed inward while the pathogen sits on the surface. 互代則方義全變。風寒初起而予乾薑者，邪在表而溫其裡。"},{"t":"Fresh warms the interior and dried releases the exterior","c":"生者溫裡，乾者解表","ok":0,"note":"Exactly reversed. Fresh is the lighter, outward one — which is also why it is the one you put in a soup. 適得其反。生者性輕而外達，故亦為湯中所常置。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Raw Rehmannia and Prepared Rehmannia — what does the steaming change?","qZh":"生地黃與熟地黃，蒸製所變者何？","opts":[{"t":"Cold becomes slightly warm; cooling blood and generating fluids becomes nourishing blood and filling essence","c":"由寒轉微溫；由清熱涼血、生津轉為養血滋陰、補精填髓","ok":1,"note":"One of the cleanest processing contrasts in the materia medica, and a standing exam favourite. Raw 生地黃 cools blood and clears heat in warm-disease patterns. Prepared 熟地黃, steamed until dark and glossy, is the anchor of Liu Wei Di Huang Wan and Si Wu Tang — it fills. Same root, opposite job. 炮製之辨，此最明白，亦考試所恆問。生地清熱涼血，溫病所用。熟地九蒸而黑潤，為六味地黃丸、四物湯之君，其功在填。同根而異用。"},{"t":"It becomes stronger at draining damp","c":"利濕之力更強","ok":0,"note":"The opposite — preparation makes it richer and more cloying, which is why it is paired with Chenpi or Sharen. 適相反。製後益滋膩，故每配陳皮、砂仁以行之。"},{"t":"It becomes an exterior-releasing herb","c":"轉為解表之品","ok":0,"note":"Neither form releases the exterior. 生熟俱不解表。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Cinnamon twig and Cinnamon bark — which is which, and why does it matter?","qZh":"桂枝與肉桂，孰為枝、孰為皮？其別何以要緊？","opts":[{"t":"Guizhi is the young twig and releases the exterior; Rougui is the trunk bark and warms the interior, supplementing the fire of the gate of vitality","c":"桂枝為嫩枝，解表；肉桂為幹皮，溫裡而補命門之火","ok":1,"note":"Thin and outward versus thick and downward — the part of the plant tells you the direction. Guizhi opens the surface and warms the channels; Rougui goes to the root, warms the Kidney, disperses cold pain and leads floating fire back down. Students lose marks on this pair every year. 細而外達，厚而下沉——取其何部，即知其何向。桂枝解肌溫經；肉桂入下焦，溫腎、散寒止痛、引火歸元。此對年年有人失分。"},{"t":"Rougui is the twig and Guizhi is the bark","c":"肉桂為枝，桂枝為皮","ok":0,"note":"Reversed. The names carry it: 枝 is a branch, 桂 alone with 肉 is the fleshy bark. 適相反。其名自明：枝者branch，肉桂者其厚皮也。"},{"t":"They are two names for the same medicinal","c":"一物二名","ok":0,"note":"Not interchangeable. Giving Rougui where a formula asks for Guizhi warms the root instead of opening the surface. 不可互代。方求桂枝而予肉桂者，溫其本而不開其表。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Ginseng and Codonopsis are both qi tonics. When must it be Ginseng?","qZh":"人參與黨參俱為補氣之品。何時非人參不可？","opts":[{"t":"In collapse — when original qi is failing and the pulse is faint; Codonopsis is gentler and cannot rescue","c":"氣脫危候：元氣將絕、脈微欲絕之時。黨參力緩，不能救脫","ok":1,"note":"The axis is strength, and it only matters at the extreme. Ginseng 大補元氣 and is the herb of Du Shen Tang for collapse. Codonopsis supplements the middle and boosts qi for ordinary Spleen-Lung deficiency, and in many everyday formulas it substitutes for Ginseng at two to three times the dose. In an emergency it cannot. 其軸在力，而其別惟見於極處。人參大補元氣，獨參湯救脫之品。黨參補中益氣，日常脾肺氣虛可代人參，用量倍之或三倍。至於危急，則不能代。"},{"t":"Whenever the patient can afford it","c":"凡病家力能負者","ok":0,"note":"Cost is not a pattern. Ginseng in a full, hot body makes it worse. 貴賤非證也。實而熱者服之更甚。"},{"t":"Whenever there is yin deficiency with heat","c":"凡陰虛火旺者","ok":0,"note":"That is when Ginseng is used with caution, not preference — it is warm. 此正人參當慎之時，非當先之時：其性溫也。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Ginseng and Astragalus are both qi tonics. What does Astragalus do that Ginseng does not?","qZh":"人參與黃耆俱補氣。黃耆能而人參不專者為何？","opts":[{"t":"It raises sunken yang and secures the exterior, stopping spontaneous daytime sweating","c":"升舉下陷之陽氣，固表止自汗","ok":1,"note":"The axis here is direction, not strength. Astragalus tonifies <i>and</i> lifts, and tonifies <i>and</i> guards the surface — which is why it is chief in Buzhong Yiqi Tang and in Yu Ping Feng San. Ginseng fills the deep reserve but does not reach the wei qi at the border. 此對之軸在向而不在力。黃耆補而能升、補而能固表，故為補中益氣湯與玉屏風散之君。人參補其元而不專達衛表。"},{"t":"It greatly tonifies original qi in collapse","c":"大補元氣，救脫","ok":0,"note":"That is Ginseng, and the distinction matters in an emergency. 此人參之長，危急之際不可混。"},{"t":"It nourishes yin and generates fluids","c":"滋陰生津","ok":0,"note":"Astragalus is warm and moves upward and outward; it is not a yin herb. 黃耆性溫升散，非滋陰之品。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Danggui and Chuanxiong both enter the blood. How do they divide?","qZh":"當歸與川芎俱入血分。其分何在？","opts":[{"t":"Danggui supplements blood and also moves it; Chuanxiong moves blood and moves qi, and rises to the head","c":"當歸補血而兼行血；川芎行血兼行氣，且上達巔頂","ok":1,"note":"Danggui is the blood tonic that happens to move — 補血活血, which is why it suits menstrual disorders where the blood is both short and stuck. Chuanxiong is called 血中之氣藥, the qi-herb within the blood; it does not supplement, it pushes, and it ascends, which is why the old line says no headache formula is without it. 當歸為補血之品而兼能行，故宜於血虛而兼滯之經病。川芎稱「血中之氣藥」，不補而但行，且性升，故有「頭痛不離川芎」之語。"},{"t":"Danggui moves blood and Chuanxiong supplements it","c":"當歸行血，川芎補血","ok":0,"note":"Reversed. Chuanxiong is acrid, warm and dispersing — it has no filling action at all. 適相反。川芎辛溫走散，全無填補之力。"},{"t":"Both are purely blood tonics","c":"二者俱為純補血之品","ok":0,"note":"Then neither could be used for stasis, and both routinely are. 若然，則二者皆不可治瘀，而實則常用之。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Baizhu and Fuling both handle damp. How do they differ?","qZh":"白朮與茯苓俱治濕。所異者何？","opts":[{"t":"Baizhu is warm and bitter — it dries damp and strengthens the Spleen; Fuling is bland and neutral — it percolates damp out through the urine and also quiets the spirit","c":"白朮苦溫，燥濕健脾；茯苓甘淡平，利水滲濕，兼能寧心安神","ok":1,"note":"Drying versus draining, and it decides which one leads. Baizhu works on the Spleen&#39;s transforming function so less damp is made. Fuling gives the damp that already exists a way out, and its extra talent — calming the spirit — is why it appears in formulas for palpitations and poor sleep. They are constantly used together because the two actions are complementary. 燥與滲之別，決其孰為君。白朮扶脾之運，使濕不復生；茯苓予已成之濕以出路，且兼寧心，故心悸失眠之方多有之。二者功相補，故每同用。"},{"t":"Baizhu drains through the urine and Fuling dries","c":"白朮利小便，茯苓燥濕","ok":0,"note":"Reversed. Fuling is the bland percolating one; that blandness is its whole character. 適相反。茯苓甘淡滲利，淡者其本性也。"},{"t":"Fuling strengthens the Spleen and Baizhu calms the spirit","c":"茯苓健脾，白朮安神","ok":0,"note":"Both halves are swapped. Baizhu has no spirit-calming action. 二者俱易其位。白朮無安神之功。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Fuling and Yiyiren both drain damp. Which suits a hot, swollen, aching joint better, and why?","qZh":"茯苓與薏苡仁俱能利濕。關節紅腫熱痛者，孰為宜？何故？","opts":[{"t":"Yiyiren — it is slightly cool rather than neutral, and it specifically eases damp obstruction in the sinews","c":"薏苡仁 — 性微寒而非平，且專能舒筋除痹","ok":1,"note":"Temperature and territory both favour it. Fuling is neutral and general; Yiyiren is slightly cooling and has a stated action on damp bi in the sinews, which is exactly a damp-heat joint. Bland and slow, so it is used in quantity and over time. Traditionally used with caution in pregnancy. 寒溫與所主俱宜。茯苓性平而其用泛；薏苡仁微寒，且本有舒筋除痹之功，正合濕熱之痹。其性淡緩，故當多用久服。孕婦慎之。"},{"t":"Fuling, because it is stronger","c":"茯苓，以其力大","ok":0,"note":"Strength is not the axis. Fuling is neutral and does not address the heat or the sinews. 其軸不在力。茯苓性平，於熱與筋俱無所主。"},{"t":"Neither — damp-draining herbs are not used in joint disease","c":"二者皆非 — 痹證不用利濕之品","ok":0,"note":"Damp bi is one of the four kinds of bi, and draining damp is central to treating it. 著痹為四痹之一，利濕正其治之要。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Zhizi and Danzhuye both clear heat and relieve restlessness. What is Zhizi&#39;s extra talent?","qZh":"梔子與淡竹葉俱能清熱除煩。梔子所獨者何？","opts":[{"t":"It drains fire downward and out through the urine, so it also resolves damp-heat jaundice","c":"導熱下行從小便而出，故兼能清利濕熱而退黃","ok":1,"note":"Direction is the difference. Both settle vexation, but Zhizi carries heat downward and out, which is why it colours the urine and why it is the minister herb in Yinchenhao Tang for bright yellow jaundice. Danzhuye clears Heart heat and promotes urination for mouth ulcers and restlessness, without the jaundice indication. 其別在向。二者俱能除煩，而梔子導熱下行而出於溲，故小便變色，亦所以為茵陳蒿湯之臣而能退黃。淡竹葉清心利尿，主口瘡心煩，而無退黃之用。"},{"t":"It nourishes yin","c":"能滋陰","ok":0,"note":"Zhizi is bitter and cold — draining, not filling. 梔子苦寒，主瀉不主補。"},{"t":"It is safe in Spleen deficiency with loose stools","c":"脾虛便溏者可安用","ok":0,"note":"The opposite: bitter and cold injures the middle, and that is exactly when Zhizi is avoided. 適相反：苦寒傷中，此正梔子所忌。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Maidong and Baihe both moisten the Lung. Which one is reached for when the patient cannot settle after a febrile illness, and why?","qZh":"麥門冬與百合俱能潤肺。熱病之後心神不寧者，當取何者？何故？","opts":[{"t":"Either can serve, but Baihe is the one named for clearing Heart heat and calming the spirit in lingering unrest after a fever; Maidong leads with moistening the Lung and generating Stomach fluids","c":"二者皆可，而百合本主清心安神，用於熱病餘熱未清、神不安者；麥冬則以潤肺、益胃生津為主","ok":1,"note":"They overlap heavily and the emphasis differs. Baihe 百合 moistens the Lung, clears Heart heat and calms the spirit — 百合病 is literally named for that restless aftermath. Maidong 麥門冬 moistens the Lung, benefits the Stomach and generates fluids, and clears Heart heat as its third action rather than its first. When two herbs overlap, name the emphasis rather than pretending they are different. 二者所主相重而輕重有異。百合潤肺、清心、安神，「百合病」即以其後之神不安而名。麥冬潤肺、益胃生津，而清心除煩列其第三。凡二藥功近，當言其輕重，不必強分。"},{"t":"Maidong, because Baihe does not enter the Heart","c":"麥冬，以百合不入心經","ok":0,"note":"Baihe does enter the Heart; calming the spirit is one of its stated actions. 百合入心，安神即其本功。"},{"t":"Neither is suitable after a fever","c":"熱病之後，二者俱不宜","ok":0,"note":"Both are standard in exactly that situation, where fluids have been damaged. 熱病傷津之後，二者正其常用。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Danggui and Baishao are the two blood herbs of Si Wu Tang. How do they divide the work?","qZh":"四物湯中當歸與白芍，二者分工何如？","opts":[{"t":"Danggui supplements and moves the blood; Baishao nourishes blood, softens the Liver, preserves yin and relieves cramping pain","c":"當歸補血活血；白芍養血柔肝、斂陰、緩急止痛","ok":1,"note":"One moves, one gathers. Danggui is warm and moving; Baishao is sour and inward, restraining — which is why it is the herb for a cramping abdomen and for a Liver that is too tight. In one formula you have both directions, which is a large part of why Si Wu Tang holds together. 一行一斂。當歸性溫而走；白芍味酸而收，故主拘急腹痛與肝之過急。一方而具二向，四物湯之所以能立者，多在於此。"},{"t":"Both simply supplement blood, with no difference","c":"二者但補血，無所異","ok":0,"note":"Then the formula would not need both. Look for what each one adds. 若然，則方不必兼用。當求其各有所加者。"},{"t":"Baishao moves blood and Danggui restrains it","c":"白芍行血，當歸斂之","ok":0,"note":"Reversed. Sour flavour gathers; Baishao is the sour one. 適相反。酸主收，白芍者其酸者也。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Gancao and Hongzao both appear at the end of formulas. What is each actually doing there?","qZh":"甘草與紅棗每列方末。其各所司者何？","opts":[{"t":"Gancao harmonises the other herbs and moderates harshness, and tonifies Spleen qi; Hongzao supplements qi and nourishes blood, and protects the middle from harsh medicinals","c":"甘草調和諸藥、緩和藥性，兼補脾益氣；紅棗補氣養血，兼護中焦以受峻藥","ok":1,"note":"Both are envoys, and both protect the middle — but Gancao&#39;s defining action is harmonising and moderating, while Hongzao leans toward supplementing qi and blood. Note also that Gancao at high doses or over long periods has its own cautions, which is a fact worth carrying rather than treating it as merely a sweetener. 二者俱為使藥，俱能護中，而甘草之本功在調和緩急，紅棗則偏於補氣養血。又當記：甘草大量或久服自有其戒，不可但以矯味視之。"},{"t":"Both are only there to improve the taste","c":"二者但為矯味","ok":0,"note":"Taste is not why a pairing survives a thousand years of use. 古方傳世千載，不為口味。"},{"t":"Both are cooling herbs that offset warm formulas","c":"二者俱為寒藥，以制溫方","ok":0,"note":"Neither is a cooling herb; Hongzao is warm and Gancao is neutral to slightly warm when prepared. 二者俱非寒品：紅棗性溫，甘草炙則微溫。"}]}]};
